# A trimmed copy of the base FrankenPHP image's own /etc/frankenphp/Caddyfile (same
# structure, same env-var hooks), with rules that reject a request body before PHP ever
# sees it, for every route except the OCI registry.
#
# Why this file exists instead of the previous `frankenphp php-server -r public/ --listen
# :8080` command: `php-server` has no way to configure any body-size limit at all, and
# without one, PHP's own `post_max_size` is the only backstop — but that value has to be
# effectively unlimited (see docker/php.ini) because a single OCI image layer legitimately
# exceeds any sane fixed cap by a wide margin (Task 7's 500 MiB gate).
#
# What this does NOT use, and why: Caddy's own `request_body { max_size ... }` directive
# looks like the obvious fit, but it does not protect a route handled by FrankenPHP's own
# `php`/`php_server` handler. Verified directly, twice, against this exact image: (1) with
# `request_body { max_size 10 }` in front of `php_server`, a 140-byte POST to `/login`
# still reached Laravel and got a normal Laravel response — the ten-byte cap had zero
# effect; (2) even against a trivial `respond "ok"` handler that never reads the body at
# all, an oversized request still got a 200, because `request_body`'s `max_size` is a LAZY
# check (Go's `http.MaxBytesReader`): it only errors once something actually reads past the
# limit, and produces no rejection at all if nothing reads that far or if the reader it
# wrapped is never the one the eventual handler reads through.
#
# What IS used, and its one confirmed hole: a preflight check on the declared
# `Content-Length` header — answered by `respond` before any handler, PHP included, ever
# touches the connection — refuses a POST/PUT/PATCH outside `/v2/*` whose declared length
# exceeds 256 MiB (see `@oversized` below). This is real admission control, not a lazy
# reader: confirmed with the same probe used to rule out `request_body` above, a rejection
# happens with zero bytes of the body consumed. The cap sits ABOVE every application-level
# upload limit this instance configures (`config/kontorfix.php`'s `npm_max_tarball_bytes` =
# 100 MiB, `python_max_dist_bytes` = 200 MiB — and matches `upload_max_filesize` in
# docker/php.ini) on purpose: this exists to stop an absurd body from reaching PHP at all,
# not to enforce the real per-ecosystem limits, which belong to the application and come
# with a proper JSON error body — a bare 413 from Caddy has none.
#
# `Content-Length` is the one signal a preflight check has, and a CHUNKED body declares no
# length at all — this file previously carried a second rule trying to catch that case by
# checking `{http.request.header.Transfer-Encoding}` for a chunked request. That rule could
# never fire: Go's `net/http` server strips `Transfer-Encoding` from the parsed request's
# header map once it has consumed the chunked framing, so the placeholder is always empty
# by the time Caddy evaluates it — confirmed by sending a real chunked request over a raw
# socket (not curl's own framing) and reading back what both a Caddy `respond` template and
# PHP's own `$_SERVER` saw: neither ever contains it. Checked every registered Caddy
# matcher module (`frankenphp list-modules | grep http.matchers`) for anything else that
# might expose it — `client_ip`, `expression`, `file`, `header`, `header_regexp`, `host`,
# `method`, `not`, `path`, `path_regexp`, `protocol`, `query`, `remote_ip`, `tls`, `vars`,
# `vars_regexp` — none of them read anything but the same stripped header map or metadata
# unrelated to transfer encoding. There is no Caddyfile-level way to detect a chunked body.
# The rule was removed rather than left in place looking like protection it cannot provide.
#
# What actually bounds an undeclared-length (chunked) body, verified against this image
# rather than assumed, is PHP itself — NOT `post_max_size`, which was confirmed by the same
# probes to only ever produce a warning for method POST and nothing at all for PUT/PATCH,
# never truncating the read either way:
#
#   - `multipart/form-data` (the one real anonymous-adjacent upload shape in this app,
#     twine's wheel/sdist) is parsed by PHP's own C multipart parser, which enforces
#     `upload_max_filesize` DURING parsing regardless of chunking — confirmed by sending a
#     30 MiB chunked multipart file against a 20 MiB cap and observing `$_FILES` report
#     `UPLOAD_ERR_INI_SIZE`, size 0, nothing ever written to a temp file. `max_file_uploads`
#     (docker/php.ini) is pinned to 1 for the same reason: nothing in this app sends more
#     than one file per request, so there is no reason to let a client multiply that
#     per-file cap by twenty.
#   - `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` (a real login form's actual shape) is parsed into
#     `$_POST` unconditionally during PHP's request bootstrap, before any Laravel code
#     runs, chunked or not — confirmed by sending a 500 MiB chunked urlencoded body to a
#     route that never once reads `$_POST` and getting a clean PHP Fatal (`Allowed memory
#     size ... exhausted`) at exactly `memory_limit`, not at any post_max_size-related
#     boundary. `memory_limit` is what actually bounds this, and docker/php.ini now pins it
#     explicitly rather than leaving it at whatever the base image happens to compile in.
#   - Any OTHER content type (raw JSON, `application/octet-stream`, …) is never read by PHP
#     at all unless application code explicitly asks for it — confirmed by sending a 500
#     MiB chunked body of such a type to a route that touches neither `$_POST` nor
#     `php://input` and getting an instant, clean response; the bytes are never buffered
#     anywhere. Under `/v2/*`, every controller action calls `ociWritableRepository()` /
#     `ociRepository()` — which throws for an anonymous or invalid token — BEFORE it ever
#     reads the request body, so an anonymous, non-multipart push never reaches the point
#     where anything would read it either.
#
# `@ociMultipart` below closes the one gap that ordering does not: PHP's multipart parser
# runs during request bootstrap, before `registry.auth` (a Laravel middleware) gets a
# chance to run at all — so an anonymous multipart POST under `/v2/*` would otherwise still
# make PHP write to a temp file pre-auth. An OCI blob or manifest is never sent as
# multipart (the distribution spec always uses raw `application/octet-stream`/JSON), so
# refusing that content type there costs nothing real.
{
	skip_install_trust

	{$CADDY_GLOBAL_OPTIONS}

	frankenphp {
		{$FRANKENPHP_CONFIG}
	}
}

{$CADDY_EXTRA_CONFIG}

{$SERVER_NAME:localhost} {
	root {$SERVER_ROOT:public/}
	encode zstd br gzip

	{$CADDY_SERVER_EXTRA_DIRECTIVES}

	# `path_regexp`, not the bare `path` matcher, for every `/v2/` exclusion below: Caddy's
	# `path` matcher is case-insensitive, so `path /v2/*` would let a request to `/V2/foo`
	# skip both rules here — it matches no Laravel route (routes/registry.php registers the
	# lowercase form only), but it is a free bypass of caps that exist for a reason.
	# `^/v2/` is anchored and case-sensitive by default (Go regex, no `(?i)` flag).
	#
	# Scoped to POST/PUT/PATCH: a GET or HEAD (the healthcheck's `/up` among them) has no
	# body and legitimately carries no Content-Length at all. Caught while verifying this
	# file, not hypothetically: a bare `method POST PUT PATCH` line as its own matcher key,
	# sitting alongside an `expression` key in the same matcher set, took `/up` down anyway
	# — with a 500, not even a clean pass-through. Caddy's matcher set does not short-circuit
	# across matcher TYPES: the `expression` matcher still gets evaluated for a GET with no
	# Content-Length, `int("")` panics inside CEL, and that panic surfaces as a 500 before
	# "the method matcher would have said no" ever gets consulted. Folding the method check
	# into a leading `&&` term of the SAME expression (below) is what actually fixes it:
	# CEL's `&&` is specified to short-circuit, unlike Caddy's own cross-matcher-type AND.
	@oversized {
		not path_regexp ^/v2/
		expression {http.request.method} in ["POST", "PUT", "PATCH"] && {http.request.header.Content-Length} != "" && int({http.request.header.Content-Length}) > 268435456
	}
	respond @oversized 413

	@ociMultipart {
		path_regexp ^/v2/
		method POST PUT PATCH
		header Content-Type multipart/form-data*
	}
	respond @ociMultipart 415

	php_server
}
